From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] PCI/AER: skip ANFE unmask on the Apple T2 Secure Enclave (106b:1802)

eddba19b8b5f ("PCI/AER: Support Advisory Non-Fatal Errors") unmasked the
Advisory Non-Fatal Error bit in the AER Correctable Error Mask of every
device on enumeration.  On a MacBookPro16,1 (Intel Cannon Lake PCH, Apple
T2 Secure Enclave 106b:1802) doing this causes the machine to lose power
shortly after boot, before any user-space console appears.  No panic, no
oops, no log line: the box just goes dark.

Wunner's 79c168e2aa95 ("PCI/AER: Skip error recovery on false alarms",
arrive with v7.3-rc7) prevents the recovery path from running when the
Root Port raised a false Firmware First alarm, which stops the
Navi10/amdgpu probe regression Alex reported but does NOT prevent the T2
Secure Enclave from being enumerated with its ANFE bit unmasked.  This
quirk targets the SEP specifically: it leaves the SEP's ANFE bit masked
while the rest of the platform is unchanged.

The SEP is a PCIe function on the T2 chipset; on a MacBookPro16,1 the
host bridge owns the ANFE bit for child devices through the AER mask.
Leaving the SEP bit masked is what the pre-7.3 behaviour effectively was
(eddba19b8b5f is what set it).  Hardware tested 2026-10-09 on
two MacBookPro16,1 units as a 7.3.0-rc6 test kernel (GUI,
suspend/resume, cold start confirmed, 2h+ stable) on top of Wunner's
79c168e2aa95.

The quirk is registered as DECLARE_PCI_FIXUP_EARLY so it runs before
pci_aer_init() looks at the device.  A new pci_dev.no_aer_anfe bit (1
bit) is the predicate pci_aer_init() checks; it does not touch any
existing field, struct layout or genksyms signature, so the change is
isolated to pci.h/aer.c/quirks.c.

Drop the quirk when either (a) the SEP ships firmware handling the
unmask without powering off, or (b) upstream grows a per-device ANFE
disable in pci_aer_init() with a non-quirk mechanism (an upstream
ANFE-disable-tree option would also be sufficient).

Not mainline yet: no_aer_anfe is absent from torvalds/master quirks.c,
pci.h and aer.c, and from pci.git for-linus/next as of 2026-10-11
(sha256 of the clean patch
600a28f7fce5ca7687086127e05737fbe7c7cadf9a0c06b797d5ed2f0e6faac5).
The Wunner fix that lands with v7.3-rc7 is a recovery-path guard, not a
device-enumeration guard; the T2 SEP still needs this enumeration-side
quirk.

Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/pci/pcie/aer.c b/drivers/pci/pcie/aer.c
index 922a726a52a5..732f75a31760 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/pcie/aer.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/pcie/aer.c
@@ -428,7 +428,8 @@ void pci_aer_init(struct pci_dev *dev)
 	 * Advisory Non-Fatal Errors are masked by default (PCIe r7.0, sec
 	 * 7.8.4.6).
 	 */
-	if (pcie_aer_is_native(dev) && dev->devcap & PCI_EXP_DEVCAP_RBER)
+	if (pcie_aer_is_native(dev) && dev->devcap & PCI_EXP_DEVCAP_RBER &&
+	    !dev->no_aer_anfe)
 		pci_clear_and_set_config_dword(dev,
 					       dev->aer_cap + PCI_ERR_COR_MASK,
 					       PCI_ERR_COR_ADV_NFAT, 0);
diff --git a/drivers/pci/quirks.c b/drivers/pci/quirks.c
index de9bbccda21f..677cd8797d19 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/quirks.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/quirks.c
@@ -2574,6 +2574,17 @@ static void quirk_p64h2_1k_io(struct pci_dev *dev)
 }
 DECLARE_PCI_FIXUP_HEADER(PCI_VENDOR_ID_INTEL, 0x1460, quirk_p64h2_1k_io);
 
+/*
+ * Unmasking Advisory Non-Fatal Errors on the Apple T2 Secure Enclave
+ * causes a MacBookPro16,1 to lose power.  Set this before pci_aer_init()
+ * so that it leaves the ANFE mask bit unchanged.
+ */
+static void quirk_no_aer_anfe(struct pci_dev *dev)
+{
+	dev->no_aer_anfe = 1;
+}
+DECLARE_PCI_FIXUP_EARLY(PCI_VENDOR_ID_APPLE, 0x1802, quirk_no_aer_anfe);
+
 /*
  * Under some circumstances, AER is not linked with extended capabilities.
  * Force it to be linked by setting the corresponding control bit in the
diff --git a/include/linux/pci.h b/include/linux/pci.h
index d31a8d107b1e..5ffcf5ce8c96 100644
--- a/include/linux/pci.h
+++ b/include/linux/pci.h
@@ -467,6 +467,7 @@ struct pci_dev {
 
 	unsigned int	is_busmaster:1;		/* Is busmaster */
 	unsigned int	no_msi:1;		/* May not use MSI */
+	unsigned int	no_aer_anfe:1;		/* Do not unmask ANFE */
 	unsigned int	block_cfg_access:1;	/* Config space access blocked */
 	unsigned int	broken_parity_status:1;	/* Generates false positive parity */
 	unsigned int	irq_reroute_variant:2;	/* Needs IRQ rerouting variant */
